title: The Rock is Jesus Christ, Not Peter
description: What the earliest church writers said about the rock of Matthew 16:18, set beside the King James text of Peter's confession.
output: lessons/heresies/heresies-that-rock-was-christ.html

# Converted from the hand-made page lessons/heresies/heresies-that-rock-was-christ.html (13 quote cards).
# Fact-check, using the printed English texts (New Advent / Schaff):
#  - Origen, Chrysostom, Hilary and Cyprian: the old wording was a loose paraphrase; the translated text is now quoted.
#  - Ambrose: the old line is a paraphrase of De Incarnationis Dominicae Sacramento 32-34; the sentence is now quoted.
#  - Augustine: the old text blends two places and Augustine, in Retractations I.21, gives both readings and tells the reader to choose. Noted on the slide.
#  - Theodoret, Eusebius, Clement, Jerome, Basil: no source found for the quoted words. Kept as the author has them, on a slide that says so.
#  - Tertullian ("The Rock is Christ, not Peter") and Gregory of Nyssa (a one-line summary, not a quotation) are kept as the author had them, on the "still to be sourced" slide.
#    Tertullian, Prescription Against Heretics 22, himself calls Peter "the rock on which the church should be built". Questions for the author.

=== Peter's confession
--- The Rock is Jesus Christ, Not Peter [title]
overline: Matthew 16 · Church Fathers Testimony
heading: The Rock is Jesus Christ, Not Peter
sub: What the earliest church actually taught
scripture: Matthew 16:15-18

=== Witnesses with a source
--- Origen of Alexandria
kicker: 1 · c. 184–253 A.D.
quote: And if we too have said like Peter, “Thou art the Christ, the Son of the living God,” … we become a Peter, and to us there might be said by the Word, “Thou art Peter,” etc. For a rock is every disciple of Christ. | Origen of Alexandria, c. 184–253 A.D., Commentary on Matthew, XII.10

--- St. John Chrysostom
kicker: 2 · c. 349–407 A.D.
quote: You are Peter, and upon this rock will I build my Church; that is, on the faith of his confession. | St. John Chrysostom, c. 349–407 A.D., Homily 54 on Matthew

--- St. Hilary of Poitiers
kicker: 3 · c. 310–367 A.D.
quote: And this is the rock of confession whereon the Church is built. … This faith it is which is the foundation of the Church; through this faith the gates of hell cannot prevail against her. | St. Hilary of Poitiers, c. 310–367 A.D., On the Trinity, VI.36–37

--- St. Ambrose of Milan
kicker: 4 · c. 339–397 A.D.
quote: Faith, then, is the foundation of the Church, for it was not said of Peter’s flesh, but of his faith, that “the gates of hell shall not prevail against it.” | St. Ambrose of Milan, c. 339–397 A.D., On the Sacrament of the Lord’s Incarnation

--- St. Augustine of Hippo
kicker: 5 · 354–430 A.D.
quote: Christ did not say: you are the rock, but you are Peter. The rock was Christ. Peter was called rock because he confessed the Rock. The rock is the confession of faith Peter made: ‘You are the Christ, the Son of the living God.’ | St. Augustine of Hippo, 354–430 A.D.
note: Augustine gives this reading in his Retractations (I.21), where he says he has also taken the rock to mean Peter, sets the two side by side, and leaves the reader to choose.

--- Cyprian of Carthage
kicker: 6 · c. 200–258 A.D.
quote: Assuredly the rest of the apostles were also the same as was Peter, endowed with a like partnership both of honour and power. | Cyprian of Carthage, c. 200–258 A.D., On the Unity of the Church, 4

=== Witnesses awaiting a source
--- Quotations still to be sourced
note: The sayings that follow are as the old page had them. No printed source has yet been found for the exact words.
quote: Blessed are you, Simon Bar-Jonah because you confessed the Truth. I say to you that you are Peter, and upon this rock of the confession of faith I will build My Church. | Theodoret of Cyrus, c. 393–458 A.D. (source not yet found)
quote: The Church is built upon this confession of faith. | Eusebius of Caesarea, c. 260–339 A.D. (source not yet found)
quote: Christ is called the Rock… because He is the foundation of all teaching. | Clement of Alexandria, c. 150–215 A.D. (source not yet found)
quote: Upon this rock of confession the Church shall be built. | Jerome, c. 347–420 A.D. (source not yet found)
quote: Do not say that the Church was built upon Peter… it was built upon the faith. | Basil of Caesarea, c. 329–379 A.D. (source not yet found)
quote: His Christocentric theology excludes the idea that the foundation of the Church is a man. He refers to Christ as the Rock, not Peter. | Gregory of Nyssa, c. 335–395 A.D. (a summary, not a quotation; source not yet found)
quote: The Rock is Christ, not Peter. | Tertullian, c. 155–240 A.D. (source not yet found)
